## 📊 Results

The Spotify Songs Genre Segmentation project demonstrates the application of Unsupervised Machine Learning techniques to group songs based on their audio characteristics.

The project analyzes various Spotify audio features to identify similarities between songs and discover meaningful clusters with similar musical characteristics.

### Key Results

- Successfully performed data cleaning and preprocessing.
- Conducted Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) to understand the distribution of Spotify audio features.
- Analyzed important musical attributes such as danceability, energy, loudness, acousticness, instrumentalness, valence, and tempo.
- Applied feature scaling to prepare numerical features for clustering.
- Used K-Means Clustering to group songs based on their audio characteristics (if implemented).
- Visualized the generated clusters to understand similarities and differences between song groups.
- Identified patterns and relationships among different musical features.

### Model Evaluation

The clustering performance can be evaluated using the following metrics:

| Evaluation Metric | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Silhouette Score | Measures how well-separated the clusters are |
| Davies-Bouldin Index | Measures similarity between clusters |
| Inertia (WCSS) | Measures within-cluster variation |
| Elbow Method | Helps determine an appropriate number of clusters |

### Conclusion

The project demonstrates how Unsupervised Machine Learning can be used to discover hidden patterns in music datasets and group songs according to their audio characteristics.

The resulting clusters can help understand musical similarities and provide a foundation for developing intelligent music recommendation and playlist organization systems.

